- Retirement contributions: each 401(k) or Roth 401(k) row asks "When do you pay the tax on it?" (Pay the tax later, Pay the tax now, Some of each); some of each saves two rows with the employer's match kept on the pay-the-tax-later one (a $0 "Roth 401(k) at work" is added on Net worth when there is none), says what paying it all later would save in this year's federal tax, gives the rule of thumb with its source, the catch-up rule for people 50 and over paid over $150,000, and after saving this year's federal tax and the mix of your money when retirement starts; Paycheck shows the Roth 401(k) line from these rows and Work benefits counts both rows toward the full match.
